{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-104399" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}$ a potential function?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-104399" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{e}^{xy})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{xy})\\,j \\\\  & ={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\,i+(xy+1){e}^{xy}\\,j \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\,i+(xy+1){e}^{xy}\\,j.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-104399" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8d939fc8a531e6eeaca09394abc20319623a5be48444362bab1e4d052412a75b"} {"question" "q-104399" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-104399" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(y+1){e}^{xy}\\,i+x{e}^{xy}\\,j"] ["math" "xy{e}^{xy}\\,i+(x+1){e}^{xy}\\,j"] ["math" "y{e}^{xy}\\,i+xy{e}^{xy}\\,j"] ["math" "{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\,i+(xy+1){e}^{xy}\\,j"] ["math" "{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\,i+{x}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\,j"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-104399" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\,i+(xy+1){e}^{xy}\\,j"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-104398" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-3y)$ a potential function?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-104398" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-3y))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-3y))\\,j \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,i-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,j \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,i-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,j.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-104398" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8d939fc8a531e6eeaca09394abc20319623a5be48444362bab1e4d052412a75b"} {"question" "q-104398" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-104398" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-3y)\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,j"] ["math" "-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,i-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-3y)\\,j"] ["math" "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,i-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-3y)\\,j"] ["math" "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,j"] ["math" "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,i-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,j"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-104398" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,i-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,j"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-98947" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2}$ a potential function?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-98947" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =2xyz\\,i+{x}^{2}z\\,j+({x}^{2}y+2z)\\,k \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=2xyz\\,i+{x}^{2}z\\,j+({x}^{2}y+2z)\\,k.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-98947" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8d939fc8a531e6eeaca09394abc20319623a5be48444362bab1e4d052412a75b"} {"question" "q-98947" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-98947" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-xyz\\,i-{y}^{2}z\\,j+({y}^{2}x+2y)\\,k"] ["math" "2xyz\\,i+{x}^{2}z\\,j+({x}^{2}y+2z)\\,k"] ["math" "2xyz\\,i-{x}^{2}y\\,j+(x{z}^{2}+2y)\\,k"] ["math" "2{x}^{2}yz\\,i+{x}^{2}y\\,j-(2xy+2y)\\,k"] ["math" "xyz\\,i-{x}^{2}\\,j+4{z}^{2}\\,k"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-98947" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2xyz\\,i+{x}^{2}z\\,j+({x}^{2}y+2z)\\,k"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-104396" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y$ a potential function?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-104396" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-y)\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i-j \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=2x\\,i-j.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-104396" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8d939fc8a531e6eeaca09394abc20319623a5be48444362bab1e4d052412a75b"} {"question" "q-104396" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-104396" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2x\\,i+y\\,j"] ["math" "2x\\,i-j"] ["math" "2x\\,i-y\\,j"] ["math" "x\\,i-j"] ["math" "{x}^{2}\\,i-j"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-104396" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2x\\,i-j"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-104397" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}$ a potential function?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-104397" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})j \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,j \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,j.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-104397" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8d939fc8a531e6eeaca09394abc20319623a5be48444362bab1e4d052412a75b"} {"question" "q-104397" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-104397" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\,i+2y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\,j"] ["math" "\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,i+\\frac{2y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,j"] ["math" "\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,j"] ["math" "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,j"] ["math" "y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\,i+x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\,j"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-104397" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,j"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-123998" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F=({y}^{2}+2xy)\\,i+({x}^{2}+2axy)\\,j$ on ${R}^{2}$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-123998" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $F,$ the domain $D={R}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP={y}^{2}+2xy,\\,Q={x}^{2}+2axy.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y+2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x+2ay.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+2x & =2x+2ay \\\\ 2y & =2ay \\\\ 2y-2ay & =0 \\\\ 2y(1-a) & =0 \\\\ 1-a & =0 \\\\ a & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-123998" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8d939fc8a531e6eeaca09394abc20319623a5be48444362bab1e4d052412a75b"} {"question" "q-123998" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-123998" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-123998" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-98946" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F=(3{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi y)\\,i+(ax-\\pi x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y)\\,j$ on ${R}^{2},$ where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-98946" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $F,$ the domain $D={R}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP=3{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi y,\\,Q=ax-\\pi x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y.\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=a-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y & =a-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y \\\\ a & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-98946" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8d939fc8a531e6eeaca09394abc20319623a5be48444362bab1e4d052412a75b"} {"question" "q-98946" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-98946" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-\\pi "] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-98946" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-104410" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F=(ay-2x{y}^{3})\\,i+(x-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\,j$ on ${R}^{2},$ where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-104410" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $F,$ we have $P=ay-2x{y}^{3}$ and $Q=x-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=a-6x{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1-6x{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a-6x{y}^{2} & =1-6x{y}^{2} \\\\ a & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-104410" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8d939fc8a531e6eeaca09394abc20319623a5be48444362bab1e4d052412a75b"} {"question" "q-104410" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-104410" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-104410" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-104414" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F=\\frac{axy}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,j$ on ${R}^{2},$ where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-104414" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $F,$ we have $P=\\frac{axy}{{x}^{2}+1}$ and $Q=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{ax}{{x}^{2}+1},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{ax}{{x}^{2}+1} & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ ax & =2x \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-104414" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8d939fc8a531e6eeaca09394abc20319623a5be48444362bab1e4d052412a75b"} {"question" "q-104414" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-104414" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-104414" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-123996" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F=2ay\\,i+(3-x)\\,j$ on ${R}^{2}$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-123996" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $F,$ the domain $D={R}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP=2ay,\\,Q=3-x.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2a,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-1.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a & =-1 \\\\ a & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-123996" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8d939fc8a531e6eeaca09394abc20319623a5be48444362bab1e4d052412a75b"} {"question" "q-123996" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-123996" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-123996" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-81745" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $F=({x}^{2}+2y)\\,i+({x}^{2}+2y)\\,j$\n- $G=(y{e}^{x}+2)\\,i+({e}^{x}+4y)\\,j$\n- $H=y\\,i+x\\,j$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-81745" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P={x}^{2}+2y$ and $Q={x}^{2}+2y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $F$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=y{e}^{x}+2$ and $Q={e}^{x}+4y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{x},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=y$ and $Q=x.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-81745" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8d939fc8a531e6eeaca09394abc20319623a5be48444362bab1e4d052412a75b"} {"question" "q-81745" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-81745" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-81745" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-81460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $F(x,y)=2xy\\,i+{x}^{2}\\,j$\n- $G(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})\\,i+2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})\\,j$\n- $H(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}\\,i+x{e}^{xy}\\,j$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-81460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields.\n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=2xy$ and $Q={x}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})$ and $Q=2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2}).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=y{e}^{xy}$ and $Q=x{e}^{xy}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-81460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8d939fc8a531e6eeaca09394abc20319623a5be48444362bab1e4d052412a75b"} {"question" "q-81460" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-81460" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-81460" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-104582" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following vector fields are conservative in their respective domains?\n\n- $F=(2x-4y)\\,i+(4x-8y)\\,j$\n- $G={2}^{2x+y+1}\\,i+{2}^{2x+y}\\,j$\n- $H=2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)\\,i+\\frac{2{x}^{2}y}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,j$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-104582" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields.\n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=2x-4y$ and $Q=4x-8y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-4,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $F$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P={2}^{2x+y+1}$ and $Q={2}^{2x+y}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={2}^{2x+y+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={2}^{2x+y+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)$ and $Q=\\frac{2{x}^{2}y}{{y}^{2}+1}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{4xy}{{y}^{2}+1},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{4xy}{{y}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-104582" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8d939fc8a531e6eeaca09394abc20319623a5be48444362bab1e4d052412a75b"} {"question" "q-104582" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-104582" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-104582" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-104570" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $F=x{e}^{xy}\\,i+y{e}^{xy}\\,j$\n- $G=(xy+{y}^{2})\\,i+(xy+{x}^{2})\\,j$\n- $H=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\,j$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-104570" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=x{e}^{xy}$ and $Q=y{e}^{xy}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={x}^{2}{e}^{xy},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $F$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=xy+{y}^{2}$ and $Q=xy+{x}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x+2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=y+2x.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $G$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)$ and $Q=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y).\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-104570" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8d939fc8a531e6eeaca09394abc20319623a5be48444362bab1e4d052412a75b"} {"question" "q-104570" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-104570" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of those listed"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-104570" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-123984" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $F=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,j$\n- $G=3{e}^{3x+2y}\\,i+2{e}^{3x+2y}\\,j$\n- $H=2xy\\,i+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,j$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-123984" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ and $Q=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $F$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=3{e}^{3x+2y}$ and $Q=2{e}^{3x+2y}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=6{e}^{3x+2y},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=6{e}^{3x+2y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=2xy$ and $Q={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-123984" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8d939fc8a531e6eeaca09394abc20319623a5be48444362bab1e4d052412a75b"} {"question" "q-123984" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-123984" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "q-123984" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/state.json" "file_sha256" "366306be3565292cb5290f7298be3526f00b328f12fc93c86c786ce0010d9299"} {"question" "e-8751" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which vector field is $f(x,y) = \\sin(2x-y)$ a potential function?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/example-8751.json" "file_sha256" "1a1a7333e995ffcb66af09f7067a97113548cdf8f17af2921dd592bd51be36c8"} {"question" "e-8751" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A vector field $\\mathbf F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $\\mathbf F = \\nabla f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $\\mathbf F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y))\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y)\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y)\\,j \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F = 2\\cos(2x-y)\\,\\mathbf i -\\cos(2x-y) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/example-8751.json" "file_sha256" "1a1a7333e995ffcb66af09f7067a97113548cdf8f17af2921dd592bd51be36c8"} {"question" "e-9500" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F = \\dfrac{2ay}{1+9x^2} \\, \\mathbf i + \\arctan{3x} \\, \\mathbf j$ on $\\mathbb R^2$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/example-9500.json" "file_sha256" "9f3784808ec4b52799246dcaf91b0cf732ab0b1a41f5e6c06e4eb4a042292f75"} {"question" "e-9500" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i+ Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j$ be a vector field with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2,$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$\\mathbf F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $\\mathbf F,$ the domain $D = \\mathbb R^2,$ and we have\n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac{2ay}{1+9x^2}, \\qquad Q = \\arctan{3x}.\n$$\n\n \nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{2a}{1 + 9x^2}, \n\\qquad \n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{3}{1 + 9x^2}.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on $\\mathbb R^2.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2a}{1+9{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{3}{1+9{x}^{2}} \\\\ 2a & =3 \\\\ a & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a = \\dfrac{3}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/example-9500.json" "file_sha256" "9f3784808ec4b52799246dcaf91b0cf732ab0b1a41f5e6c06e4eb4a042292f75"} {"question" "e-9499" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on $\\mathbb R^2?$\n\n- $\\mathbf F = (e^{y^2} - 2xy) \\, \\mathbf i + (2xye^{y^2} - y) \\,\\mathbf j$\n\n- $\\mathbf G = (3x^2 - y^2) \\, \\mathbf i + (4 - 2xy) \\,\\mathbf j$\n\n- $\\mathbf H = (xy^2 + 1) \\, \\mathbf i + 2xy \\,\\mathbf j$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/example-9499.json" "file_sha256" "c4fa83aeec7a8e8a3d0510bebd49ebf2e1f0b266e4132603078b3cf6d28cf332"} {"question" "e-9499" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i+ Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j$ be a vector field with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2,$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$\\mathbf F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D = \\mathbb R^2.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $\\mathbf F,$ we have $P = e^{y^2} - 2xy$ and $Q = 2xye^{y^2} - y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 2ye^{y^2} - 2x, \\qquad  \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 2ye^{y^2}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $\\mathbf F$ is not conservative on $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\n- For the vector field $\\mathbf G,$ we have $P = 3x^2 - y^2$ and $Q = 4 - 2xy.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = -2y, \\qquad  \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = -2y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on $\\mathbb R^2,$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{G}$ is conservative on $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\n- For the vector field $\\mathbf H,$ we have $P = xy^2 + 1$ and $Q = 2xy.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 2xy, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 2y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $\\mathbf{H}$ is not conservative on $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045943/example-9499.json" "file_sha256" "c4fa83aeec7a8e8a3d0510bebd49ebf2e1f0b266e4132603078b3cf6d28cf332"}] "usage" {"q-104570" [] "e-9499" [] "e-9500" [] "e-8751" [] "q-104399" [] "q-98947" [] "q-81745" [] "q-123996" []} "basis" 1679 "retractions" [[17592186166981 :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x, y) = ye^{xy}$ a potential function?"] [17592186167473 :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x, y, z) = x^{2}yz + z^{2}$ a potential function?"] [17592186167028 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F = 2ayi + (3 - x)j$ on $R^{2}$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find 